# agent — Integration Layer

Wires Member 1's memory system and the existing `rag` package into the
Vellora MCP server. This is the piece that was missing from `context_eval`
alone: `context_eval` proves the four context strategies work in isolation,
`agent` is where they actually get used by something that talks to a user.

## Files

- **`memory_adapter.py`** — drop-in replacement for the MCP server's old
  `mcp_server/memory/episodic_memory.py` (`maybe_remember`, `load_memory_context`).
  Same call signatures, backed by `memory.memory_manager.MemoryManager`
  instead. One `MemoryManager` per `entity_id` (e.g. `"batch_1042"`), matching
  the original per-entity scoping.

- **`rag_adapter.py`** — wires `rag.factory.RAGFactory` behind a stable
  `PolicyRAG.ask(query)` interface. Requires a real LLM and (for the vector
  store) HuggingFace embeddings, neither reachable from this sandbox — see
  the module docstring for how to instantiate it in a real environment.

- **`agent.py`** — `VelloraAgent`, the actual integration point:

  ```
  User
    |
  VelloraAgent.handle_turn(user_message)
    |
  MemoryManager.get_context(strategy=...)   -> ShortTermMemory.apply_strategy()
    |
  PolicyRAG.ask(user_message)                -> rag.factory.RAGFactory pipeline
    |
  build_prompt(memory_context, rag_answer, sources, user_message)
  ```

  RAG is optional (`VelloraAgent(rag=None)` still runs the full memory half
  of the pipeline), so the integration can be exercised without a live LLM.

- **`demo.py`** — runnable end-to-end demo (`python -m agent.demo`). Memory
  is the real `MemoryManager`; RAG is `MockPolicyRAG`, a stand-in that
  implements the same `.ask()` interface with a tiny in-memory policy lookup
  instead of real vector retrieval, since this sandbox can't reach
  HuggingFace or an LLM. Swap it for `rag_adapter.build_rag(real_llm, ...)`
  in production — nothing else in `VelloraAgent` changes.

## Migration required in `mcp_server/tools.py`

```diff
- from .memory.episodic_memory import (
-     maybe_remember,
-     load_memory_context,
- )
+ from agent.memory_adapter import maybe_remember, load_memory_context
```

No other changes needed in `tools.py` — every call site
(`maybe_remember(turn_text=..., entity_id=...)` in `change_batch_status` and
`add_quality_test`, `load_memory_context(entity_id=..., opening_message=query)`
in `get_batch_memory`) already matches this module's signatures.

## What running the demo caught

Building this against the real memory package (rather than just describing
it) surfaced three real bugs, now fixed:

1. `ShortTermMemory.strategy_zone_based_pruning` required a `zones` argument
   with no default, even though it already merges with sensible per-role
   defaults internally — any caller not passing `zones={}` explicitly would
   crash. Now defaults to `None` → the built-in defaults.

2. `maybe_remember`'s first draft routed tool events through
   `PromoteDropRouter`, the same importance heuristic used for organic chat
   messages. That heuristic only gives role-based bonuses to
   `user`/`tool`/`assistant` messages, not `system` — so a real batch status
   change or failed QA test would usually score under the 0.6 default
   threshold and be silently dropped. Tool events now write directly to
   episodic memory, since the MCP tool call itself is already the "this
   matters" decision.

3. `MemoryManager.add_message` appended to a `maxlen`-bounded deque *before*
   checking whether the buffer was full, so the oldest message could be
   silently evicted by the deque itself before the router ever got a chance
   to route it. Fixed to route first when already at capacity, then append.

## Known gaps

- `rag_adapter.py` is real wiring against the actual `rag/` classes, but
  it's untested here — verify it in an environment with network access to
  HuggingFace and an LLM before treating it as proven.
- Consolidation (episodic → semantic) is periodic by design, not real-time —
  see `demo.py`'s "forcing a consolidation pass" section for why a
  just-recorded event may not show up as a semantic fact until the next
  scheduled pass (or a manual `MemoryManager.run_consolidation_now()`).
